`run12` fragments ever written) and stopped cleanly before any other work. **The console-EOF race was root-caused for real this session, not just retried.** An isolated warm stop/start repro harness (outside `bakeoff.py`, no barrier fds needed) reproduced it at a much higher, directly measurable rate than the previously-documented 1-in-12 -- roughly 30-60% per attempt across several 8-15-attempt batches. Two leading alternative theories were tested and disproved with direct evidence: (a) *node death* -- a raw guest `ps -eLo pid,ppid,...` trace spanning full failure windows showed the qemu-x86_64-translated IOL process for both nodes keeps the identical `pid` throughout every reproduced failure, alive and never torn down; (b) *pure Python GIL contention* -- a variant using real OS processes (`multiprocessing`, no shared GIL) for the two console readers instead of threads still reproduced the race at a similar order of magnitude (3/10), showing the drop is not merely a client-threading artifact. The failure consistently lands in the same real-time window (~9.7-10.9s from `lab.start`) successful boots reach their prompt in, with hundreds of already-received frames beforehand (not "before any frame" as `a9f07c5` had assumed) and often a burst of near-simultaneous `LINK`/`LINEPROTO` state-change lines in the tail immediately before the drop -- consistent with (though not proven against the actual Go supervisor, since no Go toolchain was available on the Mac to instrument `consoleHub`'s non-blocking, drop-on-full `hubClientQueue` policy directly) a burst of small pty writes racing the server's per-client console output queue, independent of client speed or node health. **Fixed at the client layer** (`tools/phase3-baking/m3-flow.py`, commit `672a31e`): `ready_console_resilient()` reconnects a fresh console WebSocket on the exact EOF/closed symptom, but -- unlike the reverted `a9f07c5` attempt -- explicitly drains (discards) every byte already buffered on the fresh socket (the hub's replay-ring, which naturally still contains recently-seen output ahead of any new live bytes on a fresh `Subscribe`) before trusting any subsequent prompt match. Re-examining `a9f07c5`'s actual asymmetric-timing failure with this session's PID evidence shows its real defect was never "reconnecting to a phantom process" (disproved above) -- it was trusting a match anywhere in the post-reconnect stream, including that stale replay, which can itself contain an already-printed prompt line. **Verified as a real fix, not a masking retry**: 15/15 clean attempts in the repro harness (8 of which genuinely hit and recovered from the race, confirmed via drained-byte counts), with every recovered pair landing in the same normal ~9.7-10.9s band non-raced consoles show -- no asymmetric/suspiciously-fast pair recurred. A real bug in the fix itself was then found and fixed in the same commit set (`3fc8d0b`): `ready_console_resilient()` was returning `ready_console()`'s own relative duration, which resets on every retry, so a recovered race under-reported `prompt_durations` (observed live: a genuine ~15s real wait reported back as ~0.009s) even though detection and the ping batches that followed were both genuinely correct; fixed to report elapsed time from the outer function's own start across every retry. **A second, independent, 100%-reproduced real bug was then found and fixed** (`bakeoff.py`, commit `3fc8d0b`), discovered only after the console race stopped masking it: `resource_sample()`'s `GUEST_RESOURCE` snippet reads the supervisor's own root-owned (`systemd Delegate=yes`) cgroup subtree and each qemu-x86_64 child's `/proc/PID/*` entries, but was invoked via plain `limactl shell` with no `sudo` -- confirmed live and directly (a debug print of the raw pid sets showed `pre_pids=[] post_pids=[]` on a real failing cell with `console_error:0`, i.e. with the console race not even a factor) that the guest's default non-privileged user cannot read that tree and `os.walk()` silently swallows every resulting permission error, so every real automated resource sample always returned zero processes; the identical snippet run manually with `sudo` against the same live lab found both real identities reliably every time. This means the `e4dd44f` qemu-identity-name fix earlier this session was itself real and correct but was only ever verified against a manual `sudo` invocation that never exercised the actual non-privileged pipeline code path -- no real automated cell could have satisfied the Traffic rule's resource requirements since that fix landed. Fixed by adding a `sudo` parameter to `guest_json()`/`resource_sample()` (`GUEST_IDENTITY` and the schedstat preflight read only world-readable/self data and do not need it). **With both of the above genuinely fixed** (three independent real cold-boot `I1-B1` cells run this session past the console-EOF fix landing, using fresh disposable run IDs each time since a `FAIL`-finalized `bake-off-runs.log` cannot be reappended: `20260819T101624Z`, `20260819T103234Z` before the sudo fix reproducing "fewer than two qualifying translator identities" 2/2 with `console_error` both 0 and >0 -- ruling the console race in or out as the cause -- then `20260819T105450Z`/`20260819T105859Z` after the sudo fix, which got past process discovery), a **third, deeper, real timing-design issue surfaced** that this session did **not** fix: `I1-B1` failed with "pre-anchor collection did not complete strictly before batch 1 start," and a debug print of the raw values showed the sampler's `pre_anchor` `collection_end_monotonic_ns` (~87s) was **74 real seconds after** the flow child's `batch1 start_monotonic_ns` (~12.8s) -- structurally impossible if the two values shared a clock reference, since the barrier pipe handshake's own happens-before ordering (`resource_sample()` completes, *then* the ACK is written, *then* the flow's `signal_barrier()` returns and only then can batch 1 begin) guarantees the sampler's collection genuinely finishes first in real wall-clock time. Directly confirmed the cause with a minimal isolated probe on this exact host (`subprocess.run([sys.executable, "-c", "print(time.monotonic_ns())"])` from a longer-running parent): the freshly spawned child's `time.monotonic_ns()` read a small, process-local value while the parent's read a much larger one at the same real instant. **Python's `time.monotonic_ns()` provides no cross-process epoch guarantee, and does not share one across `bakeoff.py` (the sampler) and `m3-flow.py` (the flow child) on this Mac** -- a design-level flaw in the frozen plan's own Traffic timing rules (which compare sampler-process- and flow-process-recorded `monotonic_ns` values as if directly comparable) likely affecting more than just this one bracket check ((a)-(e) and (i)-(iv) all mix the two), not a test-tooling bug a local patch can safely paper over without changing what those frozen rules mean. This was left unfixed rather than forced or disguised; no cell reached PASS this session and P3-04 remains **BLOCKED**, now on this cross-process clock design issue specifically (both the console-EOF race and the resource-sample permission bug are closed). **Next session's job**: resolve the cross-process monotonic-clock design flaw through the same sol-medium propose/review contract-revision discipline used for this plan's prior frozen-rule violations (candidate directions, not evaluated: switch to a single process's clock as the sole reference by having the flow report its timestamps back through the sampler rather than recording its own, or have both sides record wall-clock `time.time()` alongside `monotonic_ns()` and use that for cross-process comparisons specifically), then re-execute the plan section 6 exact invocation for I1/I2/I3 x B1/B2/B3. P3-06 was not attempted (P3-04 has not reached PASS). Session ended cleanly: VM stopped, disk headroom ~39GiB free throughout, no processes left running on the Mac.) **Final addendum, 2026-08-19 execution session, commits `8111f80`/`b2daa5e`, real PASS reached:** the cross-process clock-domain design flaw left open by the prior addendum was fixed per a third plan-revision cycle (sol-medium propose/review, plan section 6 "Third revision note", commit `19db12a` for the contract text, `8111f80` for the implementation). Every Traffic-rule timestamp now carries an explicit `sampler_process`/`flow_child_process` clock-domain tag; rule (a)'s old direct cross-process comparison (sampler `pre_anchor.collection_end_monotonic_ns` vs. child `batch1.start_monotonic_ns`) is replaced by a causal IPC protocol (`BATCH1_STARTED` one-way notice, `POST_ANCHOR-STARTED` marker sent before the post-anchor `limactl shell` collection) whose two gap bounds are each a same-process subtraction; a new schema-required `traffic_barrier_trace` records the six-message protocol in the exact required order with per-entry clock-domain tags (implemented in `bakeoff.py`/`m3-flow.py`/`host-evidence.py`/`phase3lib.py`, including a `LineReader` that correctly handles the plan's explicitly-called-out coalesced-read hazard on the child's ack pipe). All four modules' `--self-test` entry points were run for real on the Mac target (not just locally, since Windows `select()` cannot operate on pipe file descriptors, a real POSIX-vs-Windows difference discovered this session) and passed clean. **With that fix landed, real hardware immediately surfaced a second, independent, 100%-reproducible bug**, never reached by any earlier cell in this project's history because no cell had ever gotten this far before: `fault_inventory()`'s fault-keyword regex had no word boundaries, so the key `"fault"` matched as a bare substring inside the ubiquitous benign word `"default"` (systemd `"default target"`, kernel `"Default domain type"`, dozens of occurrences on every real boot), failing the check on 100% of real cold boots regardless of whether an actual fault occurred -- confirmed live via a direct guest capture of the exact `journalctl`/`dmesg`/`systemctl show` command this function runs. Word boundaries alone were not sufficient either: the standalone word `"crash"` also appears in a universal benign kernel line, `"pstore: Using crash dump compression: deflate"` (the pstore crash-DUMP subsystem's compression choice, not an actual crash), also confirmed present on this guest's real boot output. Fixed in `bakeoff.py` (commit `b2daa5e`) with `\b` word-boundary matching plus a narrow, explicit exclusion for that one specific benign pstore line; every other occurrence of any fault keyword, including any other `"crash"`, is still a hard failure. Self-test coverage added for both the false-positive and real-detection cases. **Real hardware execution, run `20260819T114655Z`** (I1's cold-boot cells reused the already-validated fresh disk from a same-session aborted run `20260819T113405Z` whose I1-PROVISION was genuine and complete -- confirmed via a byte-diff of the driver source against the frozen commit before reuse; I2 and I3 were each freshly provisioned from scratch in this run, following plan section 4 exactly): **all nine cells (`I1-B1` through `I3-B3`) committed with real exit=0**, the canonical log finalized `verdict=PASS`, and `host-evidence.py verify` independently re-confirmed `PASS` against the finalized, read-only, sha256-sealed file (`sha256=2ea2442999037e9823d11f01b85250eaf726358ea063ca3e380664dcc353fe24`, `docs/m7-evidence/phase3/rohans-macbook-air-t8103/20260819T114655Z/qemu-user/boots/bake-off-runs.log`, byte-identical copy committed to this repo). Extracted real evidence: 9 distinct `boot_id`s (one per cold boot) and 3 distinct `machine_id`s (one per install) as required; exactly 90 total complete scored ping batches (9 cells x 10 batches, satisfying the ">= 90" PASS threshold); every cell's `traffic_barrier_trace` has exactly the required 6 entries in the required order; every cell's rule (a) same-domain gap bounds were small and comfortably inside the 1.25s limit (sampler-domain pre-anchor-to-`BATCH1_STARTED` gaps ranged ~182-233 microseconds; child-domain batch-10-end-to-`POST_ANCHOR-STARTED` gaps ranged ~91-162 microseconds, across all 9 cells); every cell's `flow_lifecycle_clock_domain` field is the literal `sampler_process`; every cell's whole-window `cumulative_cpu_ticks` was strictly positive (1129-1187 ticks). **P3-04 is PASS.** VM `iolbox-m7-native-arm64-qemu` was stopped and the session left the Mac clean; real disk headroom (`df -h /System/Volumes/Data`) stayed at 39GiB free throughout this run, no unexpected processes left running. One operational lesson from this session, unrelated to the P3-04 contract itself: a detached (`nohup ... & disown`) driver process on this Mac can appear to die silently mid-run with no error in its log -- in the one case investigated here it was actually a legitimate `set -eu` script abort following the fault_inventory bug above (not an actual kill), but the Mac's power log did show a coincidental darkwake event at a similar timestamp, so a future session hitting an unexplained silent death should check both the actual script exit path and `pmset -g log` before assuming either cause.) |
