  488: ### Why headroom does not explain the divergence
  489: 
  490: The obvious objection is that the native runs simply got a healthier Mac.
  491: The measured host state at each phase's own `host-physmem-000` /
  492: `host-swap-000` sample says the opposite:
  493: 
  494: | Run | PhysMem unused at phase start | swap used |
  495: |---|---|---|
  496: | rosetta A | 340 MB | 4093 MB |
  497: | **native B (control)** | **149 MB** | **4484 MB** |
  498: 
  499: Native run B was deliberately launched *after* both rosetta runs, under
  500: **worse** host pressure than either of them ƒ?" less free RAM and more swap in
  501: use ƒ?" and still passed 2/2 with full ping sets. Host headroom therefore does
  502: not account for the difference between the arms.
  503: 
  504: ### The honest confound, and how to close it
  505: 
  506: The two arms differ in **more than execution mode**: rosetta ran the
  507: M6-vintage launcher and the M6 payload, native ran the current-HEAD launcher
  508: and the newer owner-validated payload (which contains two netprobe console
  509: fixes the M6 payload lacks). The observed rosetta symptom ƒ?" consoles stalling
  510: after their first line, then a WebSocket EOF ƒ?" is precisely the class of bug
  511: those console fixes touch, and is also the same class Phase 6's own
  512: hand-rolled diagnostic reader reproduced.
  513: 
  514: So the rosetta result supports *either* of two readings, and this session
  515: cannot separate them:
  516: 
  517: - **(a)** four concurrent nodes under Rosetta translation exceed what this
  518:   Mac sustains ƒ?" the capacity reading; or
  519: - **(b)** the M6 payload has a multi-console defect that four nodes expose
  520:   and two nodes do not ƒ?" a build-vintage reading, already fixed at HEAD.
  521: 
  522: Reading (b) is not idle: Q1 above showed the *same* M6 artifact passing
  523: item-1's two-node console 3/3, so whatever bites here needs four consoles to
  524: appear.
  525: 
  526: **What would close it:** re-run the rosetta arm with a current-HEAD
  527: `linux-amd64` payload, so launcher and payload vintage match the native arm
  528: and only the execution mode differs. No such payload exists on the Mac today;
  529: `runtime/pack-native.sh --arch amd64` builds one (it needs
  530: `supervisor/bin/supervisor-linux-amd64` from `build-release.sh` plus the
  531: fetched vpcs binary). This was **not** attempted this session: the Mac was
  532: back to 6.7 GiB free / 97 % capacity after the four VMs these runs created,
  533: and adding a build tree plus a fifth VM was not a responsible use of the
  534: remaining disk. A cheaper partial control ƒ?" rosetta with the HEAD launcher
  535: but the same M6 payload, which would at least rule launcher vintage in or out
  536: ƒ?" was skipped for the same disk reason and is the first thing to run when
  537: space is available.
