irs, `phase3lib.LineReader`, no `resource_sample()` needed since this driver does not need traffic/schedstat data) that acks `PRE_ANCHOR`/`POST_ANCHOR` immediately and treats `BATCH1_STARTED` as the one-way notice it is (run `20260819T152650Z`, `FAIL`-finalized cleanly at this exact point, retained). (2) With that fixed, `live_inventory()` (which needs `guest-inventory.sh --mode live` to observe a genuinely running qemu process) was called *after* `live_flow()` returned -- but `m3-flow.py` unconditionally calls `lab.stop` the instant it receives `POST_ANCHOR-ACK`, so the real qemu process was always already gone by the time the inventory check ran, reproducibly failing `rc=1` even though the M3 confirmation itself completed cleanly (real hello handshake `arch:"arm64"`/`ok:true`, real 100%-success 10-batch ping proof, `prompt_durations` ~9.9-10.3s matching this hardware's known real boot timing). The `FAIL`-finalized transcript that reached exactly this defect (rc=1 from `guest-inventory.sh`, no `I3-LIVE` record ever written) is retained at run `20260819T152903Z`. Fixed by moving the live-inventory capture into the `POST_ANCHOR-STARTED`/`-ACK` window itself -- the same real window `bakeoff.py`'s `resource_sample()`/`on_start` callback already uses for its own post-anchor snapshot, which is the only point m3-flow.py's own barrier protocol guarantees qemu is still alive -- confirmed working on the very next attempt: a real `I3-LIVE` record with `assertions_passed:true`, `live_qemu:true`, and two genuine `qemu-x86_64` PIDs observed mid-flow (run `20260819T153126Z`, which then `FAIL`-finalized at the next real defect below, retained). (3) `guest_manifest()`'s persistence-manifest snippet ran via a plain non-sudo `limactl shell` against `/opt/iolbox/images/*` (mode `0750 root:root`, `guest-provision.sh`'s own real install mode) and a wrong hardcoded licence path (`/etc/iolbox/iourc`; the guest's real installed layout, confirmed live by directory listing, puts it at `/opt/