  392: | 6 | **Promote native-arm64** notwithstanding the open items | `docs/macos-m7-phase6-handoff.md`, "Owner promotion ruling" | **An override, self-described as such.** No effect on any row. |
  393: | 7 | **Owner personally validated the running native-arm64 build via its GUI** | `docs/macos-m7-phase6-handoff.md`, "Owner personally validatedƒ?Ý" | Satisfies ruling 3's separate-sign-off requirement. Produced two real netprobe fixes (`0a43e96`, `339f6b1`). Not gate evidence. |
  394: 
  395: ### The distinction this document refuses to blur
  396: 
  397: The Phase 6 handoff states the position itself, and it is worth quoting because
  398: it is the project's own words, not this document's inference:
  399: 
  400: > **This is an explicit owner override, not a mechanical PROMOTE per plan
  401: > section 13's own algorithm.** The gate ledger as it stands still contains a
  402: > real FAIL ƒ?Ý and real UNEVALUATED rows ƒ?Ý ƒ?" section 13 is explicit that "No
  403: > FAIL, UNEVALUATED, or BLOCKED gate may coexist with PROMOTE." Recording this
  404: > honestly rather than silently reclassifying those rowsƒ?Ý
  405: 
  406: So: **native-arm64 was promoted, and the ledger never computed PROMOTE.** Both
  407: are true. They are not in tension, because the owner holds an override
  408: authority the plan itself reserves. What would be false ƒ?" and what this
  409: document declines to write ƒ?" is that the ledger *mechanically* reached PROMOTE.
  410: 
  411: ### Sequencing note, recorded plainly
  412: 
  413: Plan A10.3 says: "Until promotion, `auto` continues to default to Rosetta; the
  414: test hook for native preference must be explicit." That gate was flipped at
  415: `e2ffe34` ƒ?" `auto` now prefers native-arm64 whenever native preflight passes,
  416: with explicit Rosetta fallback retained. That change shipped on the strength of
  417: override ruling 6 plus personal validation 7, **ahead of any mechanical ledger
  418: outcome**, which is the sequence ruling 3 contemplates (personal review
  419: preceding a default-behaviour change). It is recorded here so no future reader
  420: infers the code change followed a mechanical PROMOTE. It did not.
  421: 
  422: ---
  423: 
  424: ## 5. Mechanical outcome
  425: 
  426: ### 5.1 Tally
  427: 
  428: | Verdict | Count |
  429: |---|---|
  430: | PASS | 44 |
  431: | FAIL | 2 (P5-09, P7R-02) |
  432: | BLOCKED | 1 (P2-03) |
  433: | UNEVALUATED | 27 |
  434: | **Total rows** | **74** |
  435: 
  436: (P7R-01 and P7R-03 are counted in PASS; P7R-02 in FAIL. Rows whose citation is
  437: Mac-only are counted at their measured verdict and flagged in-row.)
  438: 
  439: ### 5.2 Applying section 13's rules, in the order the plan states them
  440: 
  441: **PROMOTE** requires "every gate is PASS. No FAIL, UNEVALUATED, or BLOCKED
  442: gate may coexist with PROMOTE." There are 2 FAIL, 1 BLOCKED, and 34
  443: UNEVALUATED rows. **PROMOTE is unavailable.**
  444: 
  445: **NO-PROMOTE** applies when "any evaluated gate is FAIL, including any
  446: completed run that exceeds an approved threshold." P5-09 and P7R-02 are both
  447: FAIL, and P7R-02 is exactly the named case ƒ?" a completed run exceeding an
  448: approved threshold. **NO-PROMOTE applies.**
  449: 
  450: **HOLD / EXPERIMENTAL** requires that "no gate has failed". Gates have failed.
  451: The plan adds: "HOLD cannot override FAIL." **HOLD is unavailable**, despite
  452: the 27 UNEVALUATED rows that would otherwise put the ledger here.
  453: 
  454: **BLOCKED** requires that "no gate has failed". Gates have failed, and the plan
  455: adds "BLOCKED cannot coexist with PROMOTE" and "Ordinary code defects and
  456: measured regressions are not BLOCKED." **BLOCKED is unavailable** as the
  457: ledger-level outcome, notwithstanding P2-03's genuine row-level BLOCKED.
  458: 
  459: The outcomes are mutually exclusive and exactly one survives.
  460: 
  461: ### 5.3 Outcome
  462: 
  463: > ## Mechanical outcome: **NO-PROMOTE**
  464: 
  465: This holds under multiple independent sufficient conditions, so it is robust to
  466: disagreement about any single row:
  467: 
  468: 1. **P7R-02** ƒ?" rosetta-amd64 four-node, a completed run exceeding approved
  469:    threshold 4. FAIL forces NO-PROMOTE by name.
  470: 2. **P5-09** ƒ?" four-node capacity, 2/2 hard wall in the plan-required
  471:    post-soak position. Owner-waived for Phase 5's exit, but A13 forbids a
  472:    waiver downgrading a failure.
  473: 3. **Even if both FAILs were vacated**, 27 UNEVALUATED rows and 1 BLOCKED row
  474:    remain. The best available outcome would be HOLD, which "keeps `auto` on
  475:    Rosetta". PROMOTE is unreachable by any reading of this evidence.
  476: 
  477: ### 5.4 The scoped question: "native-arm64 as the `auto` default"
  478: 
  479: Asked as posed, and answered honestly: **section 13 defines no arm-scoped
  480: mechanical outcome.** Its four verdicts are properties of the whole ledger.
  481: There is no plan-defined way to compute "PROMOTE for native-arm64 while
  482: rosetta-amd64 holds a FAIL", so any such answer is an extrapolation, not a
  483: mechanical result.
  484: 
  485: Scored anyway, as far as the plan's own vocabulary permits:
  486: 
  487: - **Rows measured specifically on the native-arm64 arm are uniformly clean**:
  488:   P4-03, P4-06, P5-05, P5-12, P7R-01 all PASS; Phase 6's single in-repo native
  489:   four-node failure is affirmatively superseded by P7R-01's 4/4.
  490: - **But native-arm64 still owns UNEVALUATED rows** ƒ?" P4-13 (`diagnose` never
  491:   run), P4-14 (loopback probe, i386 probe, Windows builds), P4-01/P4-02
  492:   (provenance), and every Phase 6 A/B row, whose deltas are undefined without a
  493:   rosetta baseline. An A/B threshold cannot be satisfied one-armed.
  494: - Therefore, **even scoped to native-arm64, the result is not PROMOTE.** It is
  495:   at best HOLD, and HOLD "keeps `auto` on Rosetta" ƒ?" which is not what the
  496:   shipped code now does.
  497: 
  498: **The honest summary:** `auto` preferring native-arm64 today rests on owner
  499: override rulings 6 and 7, not on a mechanical gate result. That is a legitimate
  500: basis under the plan's own reserved authority. It is not a PROMOTE.
  501: 
  502: ### 5.5 What this outcome does and does not authorise
  503: 
  504: Per ruling 3 and A13, even a mechanical PROMOTE would authorise only the
  505: ledger's own label. **NO-PROMOTE authorises nothing at all.** It does not
  506: reverse the owner's override, does not require reverting `e2ffe34`, and does
  507: not decide the merge-to-`main` or release-pipeline questions ƒ?" those remain
  508: separate decisions that surface to the owner. Rosetta-amd64 remains the
  509: explicit fallback path and is not being removed; P7R-02 does not change that.
  510: 
  511: ---
  512: 
  513: ## 6. What would have to change for a mechanical PROMOTE
  514: 
  515: Ordered by cost, cheapest first. Items 1ƒ?"2 are clerical and would resolve
  516: roughly half the UNEVALUATED rows without any new hardware time.
  517: 
  518: 1. **Copy the existing raw evidence off the Mac into the repository.** The M4
  519:    orchestrator output for Phase 5 items 2ƒ?"7 and every Phase 7
  520:    `evidence-m4-p7/` directory already exist and were measured; they were never
  521:    committed. This alone addresses P5-06, P5-07, P5-08, P5-09, P5-10, P5-11 and
  522:    gives P7R-01/02/03 contract-valid citations.
  523: 2. **Capture the artifacts for checks that were run but not recorded**:
  524:    Phase 4 provenance (P4-01), the build/vet/test output (P4-02), a `diagnose`
  525:    run (P4-13), loopback/i386/Windows compatibility output (P4-14), and the
  526:    post-defect rerun set (P4-15).
  527: 3. **Re-run Phase 6 to its actual contract**: ƒ%3 runs per topology **per arm**,
  528:    with node-process RSS actually sampled, and threshold files that compute
  529:    medians and deltas rather than leaving them to prose. Twelve rows depend on
  530:    this and none can move without it.
  531: 4. **Discharge P7-02's `redistribution_review_required: true`** and update
  532:    `THIRD_PARTY.md` to cover the native-arm64 profile's `qemu-user-static`
  533:    (GPL) rather than describing the Mac archive as Rosetta-only.
  534: 5. **Resolve P7R-02's confound** by re-running the rosetta arm with a
