## M3 procedure and expected observations

The M3 prompt's required Mac-host flow is:

1. Upload an image, register it, create/save a lab, start it, send console
   input, stop it, and reload it from Safari or Chrome through the launcher's
   browser/open step and the forwarded GUI port.
2. Verify host-folder sync at `~/Library/Application Support/iolbox/images`
   and `~/Library/Application Support/iolbox/labs` across launcher restart.
3. Use explicit Lima host-loopback forwards for GUI `127.0.0.1:4001`, console
   range `127.0.0.1:9000-9049`, and capture range
   `127.0.0.1:5500-5529`; verify two simultaneous consoles and loopback-only
   reachability.
4. Start live capture, obtain a non-empty structurally valid `.pcapng`, and
   either open it with installed Wireshark or provide a working browser save
   path.
5. Repeat upload/sync/capture path handling with spaces and non-ASCII
   characters in the macOS data path.

The M3 result's browser-equivalent acceptance sequence is preserved exactly
as the fallback procedure that was actually run:

```text
image upload ƒ+' image.register ƒ+' lab.saveDoc/lab.listDocs (raw YAML) ƒ+'
lab.load (structured JSON) ƒ+' lab.start ƒ+' two concurrent /console/{id}
sessions reaching an IOS prompt ƒ+' capture.start ƒ+' direct 127.0.0.1:5500
dial ƒ+' /capture/0 binary stream ƒ+' SHB/IDB/EPB and block-length validation
with at least one packet ƒ+' lab.stop ƒ+' reload and identity confirmation
```

M3 also records the exact readiness/console rules that M7 must preserve:
readiness is `GET /` with status below 500; every GUI WebSocket uses the
session cookie and same-origin `Origin`; console open sends an active wake and
re-pokes; fragmented frames are accumulated; IOS pagination is disabled with
`terminal length 0`; and a capture-port forward may need a bounded retry.

